Before After <code>Transformer.transform(data, func, registry)</code> <code>Transformer.expand(data)</code>: returns <code>None</code> (a leaf) or <code>(children, rebuild)</code> A transformer recurses through <code>registry.transform</code> The engine recurses: a transformer cannot forget the cycle check or the depth limit A dataclass is a leaf; <code>enter_dataclasses()</code> enters it A dataclass is entered by default; <code>enter_dataclasses</code> is removed <code>dfs_iterate</code> and <code>bfs_iterate</code>: a dataclass is a leaf; <code>enter_dataclasses()</code> enters it A dataclass is entered by default; <code>enter_dataclasses</code> is removed from <code>coola.iterator</code> <code>dataclasses.replace</code> rebuilds a dataclass A shallow copy with the fields set: <code>init=False</code> fields are transformed, no <code>__post_init__</code> <code>ConditionalTransformer</code> Removed: guard <code>func</code>, or register <code>IdentityTransformer</code> for the type <code>recursive_apply(data, func, registry)</code> <code>recursive_apply(data, func, registry=registry)</code> <code>KeyFilterTransformer()</code> with <code>func</code> as the key predicate <code>KeyFilterTransformer(key_predicate=...)</code>; <code>func</code> is the function of the values <code>register_transformers(mapping, True)</code> <code>register_transformers(mapping, exist_ok=True)</code> <code>RecursionError</code> for a deep structure No limit: the traversal uses an explicit stack <p>Before:</p> <pre><code>class PointTransformer(BaseTransformer[Point]):
    def transform(self, data, func, registry):
        return Point(registry.transform(data.x, func), registry.transform(data.y, func))
</code></pre> <p>After:</p> <pre><code>class PointTransformer(BaseTransformer[Point]):
    def expand(self, data):
        return [data.x, data.y], lambda xs: Point(*xs)
</code></pre> <p>A plugin of the group <code>coola.recursive.transformers</code> that implements <code>transform</code> must be ported to <code>expand</code>. To keep a dataclass as a leaf, register <code>DefaultTransformer()</code> for its type (<code>DefaultChildFinder()</code> for <code>dfs_iterate</code> and <code>bfs_iterate</code>).</p>

Before After <code>coola.iterator.dfs</code> and <code>coola.iterator.bfs</code> Removed: import from <code>coola.iterator</code> <code>BaseIterator.iterate(data, registry)</code> (DFS) and <code>BaseChildFinder.find_children</code> (BFS) <code>BaseChildFinder.find_children(data)</code>: returns the children, or <code>None</code> for a leaf <code>IteratorRegistry</code>, <code>PathRegistry</code> Removed: <code>ChildFinderRegistry</code> serves both orders <code>register_iterators</code> and <code>register_child_finders</code> <code>register_child_finders(mapping, *, exist_ok=False)</code> <code>get_default_iterator_registry()</code> <code>get_default_registry()</code> (alias <code>get_default_child_finder_registry()</code>) <code>DefaultIterator</code>, <code>IterableIterator</code>, <code>MappingIterator</code>, <code>DataclassIterator</code> <code>DefaultChildFinder</code>, <code>IterableChildFinder</code>, <code>MappingChildFinder</code>, <code>DataclassChildFinder</code> A leaf overrides <code>is_leaf</code> (BFS) A leaf returns <code>None</code> from <code>find_children</code>; an empty container returns an empty iterable <code>registry.iterate(data)</code> and <code>registry.find_children(data)</code> Removed: use <code>dfs_iterate(data, registry=registry)</code> or <code>bfs_iterate(data, registry=registry)</code> <code>dfs_iterate(data, registry)</code> and <code>bfs_iterate(data, registry)</code> (deprecated) <code>registry</code> is keyword-only Plugin groups <code>coola.iterator.dfs</code> and <code>coola.iterator.bfs</code> One group: <code>coola.iterator.child_finders</code> <code>RecursionError</code> for a <code>dfs_iterate</code> structure nested about a thousand levels No limit: both orders use an explicit stack or queue <code>range</code>, a user <code>Collection</code> and a user <code>collections.abc.Set</code> are entered They are leaves, like in <code>recursive_apply</code> (register a child finder to enter them) <code>bfs_iterate</code> could raise a false <code>Cycle detected</code> for objects built by a child finder Fixed: the ancestors are compared by identity <p>Before:</p> <pre><code>class ReversedListIterator(BaseIterator[list]):
    def iterate(self, data, registry):
        for item in reversed(data):
            yield from registry.iterate(item)
</code></pre> <p>After:</p> <pre><code>class ReversedListChildFinder(BaseChildFinder[list]):
    def find_children(self, data):
        return reversed(data)
</code></pre> <p>A child finder returns the children in the order they are visited, and the same order is used by <code>dfs_iterate</code> and <code>bfs_iterate</code>: a custom order for one traversal only is no longer possible. A plugin of the group <code>coola.iterator.dfs</code> or <code>coola.iterator.bfs</code> must register a <code>ChildFinder</code> in <code>coola.iterator.child_finders</code> instead.</p>

Before After <code>Summarizer.summarize(data, registry, depth, max_depth)</code> returns a <code>str</code> and recurses <code>Summarizer.summarize(data, config, *, compact)</code> returns a <code>str</code> (a leaf) or a <code>Node</code> (a container) <code>registry.summarize(data, depth, max_depth)</code> <code>registry.summarize(data, config=None)</code> with a <code>SummaryConfig</code> <code>SequenceSummarizer(max_items=3)</code>, <code>DefaultSummarizer(max_characters=50)</code>, <code>num_spaces=4</code> <code>summarize(data, max_items=3, max_characters=50)</code>; the indentation is 2 spaces and is not an option <code>NDArraySummarizer(show_data=True)</code>, <code>PandasDataFrameSummarizer(max_fields=2)</code> <code>summarize(data, show_data=True)</code>, <code>summarize(data, max_items=2)</code> <code>max_items=-1</code> and <code>max_characters=-1</code> for "no limit" <code>max_items=None</code> and <code>max_characters=None</code> <code>summarize(data, 2)</code> and <code>register_summarizers(mapping, True)</code> <code>TypeError</code>: <code>summarize(data, max_depth=2)</code> and <code>register_summarizers(mapping, exist_ok=True)</code> <code>max_depth=True</code> and <code>max_depth=1.5</code> accepted <code>TypeError</code> <code>BaseCollectionSummarizer</code>, <code>BaseFieldsSummarizer</code>, <code>MAX_FIELDS</code> Removed. <code>format_fields(fields, max_fields)</code> has no default for <code>max_fields</code> <code>BaseMetadataSummarizer(show_data)</code> with <code>_describe(data)</code> <code>BaseMetadataSummarizer()</code> with <code>_describe(data)</code> returning fields; a table returns <code>("columns", pairs)</code> <code>repr</code> and <code>equal</code> to write in every summarizer <code>BaseSummarizer</code> gives <code>Name()</code> and "same type" <code>RecursionError</code> for a nesting of a few hundred levels No <code>RecursionError</code>: the engine uses an explicit stack A self-referencing container is repeated up to <code>max_depth</code> <code>&lt;cycle&gt;</code> where it comes back A <code>__len__</code>, a property or a <code>__iter__</code> that raises makes <code>summarize</code> raise <code>&lt;class ...&gt; &lt;summary failed: ExcType&gt;</code> and the rest of the summary is intact <code>field(repr=False)</code> fields of a dataclass are printed They are not shown <code>UserString</code> is a list of characters, <code>MappingProxyType</code> is printed whole A leaf, and a mapping A named tuple has the labels <code>(0)</code>, <code>(1)</code> The field names A container at the display depth is <code>[0, 1, 2, 3, 4] ...</code>, its <code>str</code> is computed in full <code>[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, ...]</code>, bounded work (three levels, <code>max_items</code> items) Hidden entries: <code>...</code>; empty container: <code>&lt;class 'list'&gt; []</code>; nested levels indented by 2 then 4 <code>... (+N more)</code>; <code>&lt;class 'list'&gt; (length=0)</code>; 2 spaces per level A frame, a table or a dataset lists 10 columns It lists <code>max_items</code> (5) columns <p>A custom summarizer that returned a string only needs the new signature:</p> <pre><code>from coola.summary import BaseSummarizer, Node, SummaryConfig


class PointSummarizer(BaseSummarizer[Point]):
    def summarize(self, data: Point, config: SummaryConfig, *, compact: bool) -&gt; str:
        return f"Point(x={data.x}, y={data.y})"  # a leaf


class TreeSummarizer(BaseSummarizer[Tree]):
    def summarize(self, data: Tree, config: SummaryConfig, *, compact: bool) -&gt; Node:
        # a container: the engine recurses, summarizes the children with the summarizer of their
        # type and applies max_items, max_depth and the cycle check
        return Node(
            f"Tree (children={len(data.children)})", enumerate(data.children), len(data.children)
        )
</code></pre> <p>A summarizer must be pure and cheap: it does not call the registry, does not recurse and returns the children lazily. A plugin of the entry-point group <code>coola.summary.summarizers</code> that implemented <code>summarize(data, registry, depth, max_depth)</code> must be ported. The options that were per type (<code>max_items=3</code> for lists and 10 for dicts) do not exist any more: register a summarizer that returns a <code>Node</code> with a smaller <code>total</code> if you need it. The text of a summary is meant to be read, not parsed, and its layout changes in this release.</p>

Before After <code>BaseLoader.load(path)</code> is abstract and each loader calls <code>sanitize_path</code> <code>BaseLoader._load(path: Path)</code> is abstract; <code>load</code> is concrete and converts the path Every loader and saver implements <code>equal(other, equal_nan)</code> <code>equal</code> is concrete in <code>BaseLoader</code> and <code>BaseSaver</code>: same type and same <code>_display_fields()</code> <code>InlineDisplayMixin</code> is a base of each format It is a base of <code>BaseLoader</code> and <code>BaseSaver</code> <code>JsonLoader[T]</code>, <code>PickleSaver[T]</code>, <code>TorchLoader[T]</code> <code>BaseLoader[Any]</code> / <code>BaseFileSaver[Any]</code>: the classes are not subscripted <code>save_text(123, path)</code> writes <code>123</code>; <code>save_text(None, path)</code> writes <code>None</code> <code>TypeError</code> for anything but a <code>str</code>: call <code>str()</code> first <code>TorchLoader(**kwargs)</code>, <code>weights_only</code> read from <code>kwargs</code> <code>TorchLoader(*, weights_only=True, **kwargs)</code>; shown in the repr, compared by <code>equal()</code> <code>from coola.io import add_uuid_suffix</code>; <code>coola.io.utils</code> <code>from coola.utils.path import add_uuid_suffix</code> <code>save_*(..., lock=True)</code>, <code>BaseFileSaver.save(..., lock=True)</code> <code>lock</code> is removed (<code>TypeError</code>); no <code>.lock</code> file, no heartbeat thread Saves of one path are serialized Saves are atomic and never mix; which one wins is unspecified <code>exist_ok=False</code> without hard links is protected by the lock Two processes creating the same file at the same instant can both succeed (last wins) <code>_fsync_file</code> opens the file read-only It opens it with <code>O_RDWR</code> <p>Before:</p> <pre><code>class UpperTextLoader(BaseLoader[str]):
    def equal(self, other, equal_nan=False):
        return type(other) is type(self)

    def load(self, path):
        return sanitize_path(path).read_text().upper()
</code></pre> <p>After:</p> <pre><code>class UpperTextLoader(BaseLoader[str]):
    def _load(self, path):
        return path.read_text().upper()
</code></pre> <p>A caller that needs mutual exclusion around a read-modify-write of one file takes a lock of its own around the read and the write: <code>save</code> never provided it. A temporary file <code>.&lt;name&gt;-&lt;uuid&gt;&lt;ext&gt;</code> left by a process killed during a save is hidden and safe to delete.</p>
